  The floor is the 5% lower bound around the anchor: 0.95 x 898,336 = 853,419.2.

WHY THE METHOD BOUND IS ONE-SIDED, AND WHY A HIGH COUNT IS NOT AN ERROR

  A reader meeting a count 55% above its anchor will read it as a defect unless
  the asymmetry is spelled out, so it is (AAP 0.2.1, 0.8.3).

  The anchor was measured over a NARROWER input set than a full reactor produces.
  The expected-values table names 32 JAR producers; a full reactor produces 38.
  The six extras are tools, examples and the four connector/kafka-0-10* modules.
  Six additional JAR producers cannot yield FEWER methods, so a count above the
  anchor is the expected consequence of the reactor being full, and there is no
  upper bound to check it against.

  A count BELOW the floor is the truncation signature - the failure mode a
  lowered heap produces - and a truncated graph's silence about a module is
  indistinguishable from a clean result. That is why it stops the run, and why the
  input set is never trimmed in either direction to bring a count inside a window.

  NOW THE HONEST PART, because that rationale does not fit THIS graph.

  The rule above is the governing rule and the verdict follows it. But the
  mechanism it names is not what produced the excess here, and saying otherwise
  would be inventing a cause. PHASE 3 measures which modules actually reached this
  graph: 0 of the 38 JAR-producing modules, and the seven absent ones are
  exactly sql/connect/shims plus those very six extras. So the six modules the
  AAP's rationale credits the excess to are ABSENT from this input set. Whatever
  drives the +55.55% on methods, it is not them.

  What CAN be measured about the composition is measured, and nothing beyond it
  is asserted. STEP 13 partitions every method in the graph by namespace.

STEP 13 - Composition of the method count, by namespace
