================================================================================
PHASE 3 - PER-MODULE COVERAGE WITNESSES
================================================================================

  TWO QUESTIONS, TWO PROOFS. Conflating them produces either a false failure or a
  false pass, so they are kept apart (AAP 0.8.3).

  DELIVERY - was every JAR handed to the frontend? - is already proved, and not
  by this file. cpg-input-inventory.json's staging manifest proves it one file at a
  time: 191 inventory entries map onto 191 staged files and back, TOTAL and
  INJECTIVE IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, zero unmapped either way, all 191 digests
  re-verified from the staged path with zero mismatches. That is cited, not
  re-derived - one measurement cited twice (AAP 0.6.4).

  COVERAGE - did a module's own code reach the graph? - is this file's question,
  and it has exactly one test (AAP 0.5.1):

      A class present in that module's PRIMARY artifact - its main unclassified,
      non-'original-', non-'-tests' JAR - and ABSENT from every other module's
      artifacts.

  A class shared with a SAME-MODULE sibling still qualifies, which is what makes
  the test satisfiable for a shaded artifact and its pre-shade sibling - they share
  every class, so 'unique to that JAR' is unsatisfiable while 'unique to that
  module' is not. A class shared with ANOTHER module does not qualify. Where no
  such class exists, the module-exclusive META-INF/maven/**/pom.properties node is
  accepted and NAMED AS THE WEAKER WITNESS.

  A shared package prefix is never evidence, anywhere in this file.

STEP 14 - Witness exclusivity, RE-DERIVED from the archives themselves
  The inventory asserts which classes are exclusive to which module. Taking that
