  WHAT IS THERE. All four of the classes sql/connect/shims ships as client-only
  stubs are present, each as 2 type declarations, and each carrying a full
  implementation's worth of methods - SparkConf 298, SparkContext 1100, RDD 1022,
  JavaRDD 74. A 0-method or near-0-method type declaration is what a stub would
  look like; that is not what the graph holds.

  WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. Which archive supplied those definitions is not measurable
  and is not asserted. It does not need to be, and this is the point: the two shims
  archives were excluded from this graph's input set before the frontend ran, so no
  three-way collision with the shims stubs occurred at all. cpg-frontend.log's own
  table F measures that for this input - each of the three was overwritten exactly
  once and contained in exactly two archives, both of them module core's own shaded
  and pre-shade pair. The graph therefore holds core's classes because core's
  archives are the only ones in the input that carry them, which is a containment
  fact rather than a winner.

  org.apache.spark.unused.UnusedStubClass is included in the query for scale: 29
  type declarations. It comes from Spark's org.spark-project.spark:unused stub
  dependency and is shipped by 35 modules, and it is named so its ubiquity is not
  mistaken for a measurement error - it is also why several modules' shared-class
  counts read as 1 against nearly every other module.

