================================================================================
DIVERGENCES AND HALT-CLASS FINDINGS
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
D1 - INHERITED HALT-CLASS FINDING: the graph was not created by this run
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Owned by harness/artifacts/logs/cpg-frontend.log, which states it in full and
  reasons about why repairing it was refused. Restated here in one line because it
  bounds what this file's counts describe: the counts below belong to the graph the
  provisioning invocation wrote, not to a graph built over this run's own staged
  input set. Reported, not repaired; nothing was rebuilt or overwritten to clear it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
D2 - INHERITED RECORDED DIFFERENCE: the input set is narrower than the build
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Also owned by cpg-frontend.log. Its consequence lands squarely on THIS file, so
  it is spelled out rather than referenced: the graph's input was 62 archives from
  31 modules, while this run's build produced 191 own artifacts from 38
  JAR-packaging projects. That difference is the entire reason seven modules have
  no coverage verdict obtainable (STEP 19), and the reason no finding on this graph
  can resolve into a src/test tree - every -tests archive was excluded from it.

  This file adds one measurement to that inherited record, from the graph's own
  side: the 31 modules whose witnesses are present, and the 31 Spark descriptor
  nodes that map to exactly those modules and to no others. The graph independently
  confirms which modules reached it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
D3 - RECORDED DIFFERENCE: all three counts exceed their expected values
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  expected  : methods 898,336 (anchor) with a floor of 853,420; type declarations
              87,381; files 38,818.
  observed  : methods 1,397,339; type declarations 119,691; files 45,037,
              identical across two independent loads in two separate JVMs.
  verdict   : the method count PASSES its one-sided floor and no upper bound
              applies. The other two never halt. All three are recorded as
              expected-versus-observed in PHASE 2.
  cause     : NOT ESTABLISHED, and deliberately not guessed. The AAP's stated
              rationale for an above-anchor count is six extra JAR producers, and
              PHASE 3 measures those six as ABSENT from this input set, so that
              mechanism cannot be it. STEP 13 measures what is measurable instead -
              33.65% of methods lie outside org.apache.spark, vendored by
              shading - and stops there. The anchor's own input composition is
              recorded nowhere available to this run.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
D4 - CROSS-REFERENCE, owned elsewhere: the pom.properties fallback
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  harness/ENVIRONMENT.md states that no Spark artifact at this pin contains a
  META-INF/maven/**/pom.properties node, and therefore that the AAP's weaker
  witness was unavailable for any module. cpg-input-inventory.json measured the
  opposite and owns that recorded difference; cpg-frontend.log carries it as its own
  D4 and deliberately does not re-derive it.

  This file does not re-derive it either, and it does add a measurement of its own
  on a different question - not what the archives contain, but what the GRAPH
  contains. Load 2 found 102 pom.properties file nodes in the graph, 31 of them
  Spark module descriptors. So on this graph the weaker witness is representable and
  functional, and the one module that needs it fails it only because its archive was
  excluded from the input. Both the record's statement and these observations are
  recorded; the archive-level contradiction stays with the document that owns it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
D5 - METHOD NOTE: the inventory's groupId field is not usable for node paths
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Recorded because it changed how a measurement in this file had to be taken, and
  because a later reader repeating it would otherwise get a wrong answer. Mapping a
  module to its Maven descriptor node needs groupId and artifactId. The inventory's
  per-project groupId field does not consistently hold the project's own groupId -
  it reads 'org.scala-lang.modules' for core, 'org.rocksdb' for sql/core and
  'org.scala-lang' for common/tags, while all three artifacts' real descriptor nodes
  are under org.apache.spark. Taking that field at face value produced ten spurious
  'node absent' results on a first pass. The mapping in STEP 16 therefore keys on
  artifactId under the org.apache.spark group, which the descriptor node paths
  themselves confirm: all 31 map to a JAR-producing module and none is left over.
  No file was edited to fix this; the inventory is another lane's deliverable and
  the condition is reported rather than repaired.

================================================================================
CROSS-CHECKS
================================================================================

  A count that appears in two documents must be one measurement cited twice, never
  two measurements (AAP 0.6.4). What this file measures, and who else must agree:

  1. The graph's identity. Measured here at STEP 4 and again at STEP 11, against
     the pair cpg-frontend.log recorded at write time. MATCHES on byte size, sha256
     and dev:inode. The Stage 3 Joern runner and every Stage 5 probe query
     re-verify the same pair by the same symlink-following route.

  2. The declared dependency's own digest. cpg-frontend.log STEP 2 recorded the
     inventory at sha256 3cd4de2e...5d6f; STEP 2 here re-measured it and got the
     same value, so both files are reasoning about the same version of it.

  3. The three counts. Measured here twice, in two JVMs, identically. run-record.md
     cites them as proof and must cite THESE, not re-derive them differently.

  4. Per-module coverage. oss-scan-results/build-record.md OWNS the verdict; this
     file and cpg-input-inventory.json supply its inputs. build-record.md does not
     exist yet at the time this file is written, so no agreement with it is claimed
     here - what is claimed is that the inputs it must use are these: 38
     JAR-producing modules, 31 covered with a witness measured in the graph, 7
     with no verdict obtainable and named individually, and sql/connect/shims among
     the latter with its pom.properties node named exactly. A verdict there that
     covers all 38, or that quietly drops the seven, contradicts this measurement.

  5. Coverage against the type declarations reported here. Each covered module's
     witness is a type declaration counted in the 119,691 total, and each ABSENT
     witness is absent from it. The two axes at STEP 15 and STEP 16 agree module for
     module with zero disagreements, so the coverage verdict and the type-declaration
     count are consistent by construction rather than by assertion.

  6. The provisioning's own count of covered modules. harness/ENVIRONMENT.md reports
     '31 of 31 contributing modules covered'. This load independently reports 31
     covered, over the same 31 contributing modules - reached by different witness
     choices in several cases, since the inventory selects the lexicographically
     first exclusive class and the environment record selected others. Agreement on
     the count through different witnesses is a stronger check than agreement
     through the same ones. The difference from this file's denominator is only that
     this file reports against all 38 JAR producers rather than the 31 that
     contributed, which is why 7 appear here as no-verdict rather than not at all.

================================================================================
VERDICT
================================================================================

  PHASE 1 - the load ................ PASS. Identity re-verified against the
            recorded pair on all three fields with symlink-following measurement;
            both workspaces proved absent before use, outside the checkout, neither
            reused nor cleared; JDK major 21; heap 64 GiB proven committable and not
            lowered; loaded with importCpg, exit 0, methods 1,397,339 > 0; and the
            persisted graph byte-identical afterwards.

  PHASE 2 - the three counts ........ PASS on the one-sided method floor;
            RECORDED DIFFERENCE on all three against their expected values, with
            both values given and the asymmetry and its rationale stated. The cause
            of the excess is named as not established rather than guessed. Nothing
            was trimmed in either direction to move a number.

  PHASE 3 - coverage ................ 31 of 38 JAR-producing modules covered by a
            witness measured in the graph, on two independent axes that agree module
            for module; exclusivity recomputed from the archives with zero failures;
            7 modules named as NO VERDICT OBTAINABLE with the witness tried and the
            query run, sql/connect/shims among them with its exact pom.properties
            node; no shared package prefix used anywhere; no winner map asserted.

  OVERALL ........................... The graph is loadable, non-empty, and covers
            every module whose artifacts reached it. Two inherited conditions bound
            what that statement describes - the graph was not written by this run
            (D1) and its input set is narrower than this run's build (D2) - and both
            are carried forward with their consequences rather than smoothed over.
            The seven modules without a verdict are a consequence of D2 and are
            reported as such, not as a coverage failure and not as a pass.

  WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT DO. It judges no finding, ranks no tool, and compares
  nothing against any commercial scanner. It repairs nothing: no input set was
  trimmed, no heap lowered, no workspace reused, no graph rebuilt, and no file in
  either Spark tree edited.

